{
  "question": "When can a federal contracting officer set aside an acquisition for SDVOSB concerns?",
  "status": "answered",
  "answer": "Under federal rules, a contracting officer may set aside an acquisition for SDVOSB concerns when there is a reasonable expectation, based on market research, that at least two responsible SDVOSBs will submit offers and the award can be made at a fair market price. For non-VA procurements, the officer must first determine that the requirement is not excluded from SDVOSB contracting under 13 CFR 128.403, and must consider SDVOSB set-asides before considering small business set-asides or sole source awards.",
